      "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-2)x}=\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, calculate $A+B.$",
      "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-2)x}=\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{x}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-2)x$ gives\n\n$$\nx-4=Ax+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0)+B(0-2) \\\\ -4 & =-2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-4 & =A(2)+B(2-2) \\\\ -2 & =2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-2)x}=-\\frac{1}{(x-2)}+\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA+B=-1+2=1.\n$$",
